  "id": "Compound::DB00411 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Carbachol, a cholinergic agonist, is shown to bind to multiple muscarinic acetylcholine receptors (Chrm1 and Chrm2), which are part of the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor family. These receptors are involved in the regulation of various physiological processes, including smooth muscle contraction and neurotransmitter signaling. The evidence suggests that Carbachol exerts its effects through a common mechanism involving the activation of muscarinic receptors, which in turn activate G-proteins to modulate cellular functions. The shared involvement of Chrm1 and Chrm2 in the same pathway to Chrm3 indicates a potential role for these receptors in the same biological process, possibly in the context of cholinergic signaling or receptor regulation. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Carbachol may influence Chrm3 through a mechanism involving muscarinic receptor activation, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction between Carbachol and Chrm3.",
